+# Lint and audit the workflows themselves (issue #1076).
+#
+# All four link-foundation pipeline templates ship this file, and it is the one
+# template workflow this repository had no counterpart for. The pipeline is code,
+# and until now nothing in the pipeline was looking at it: actionlint ran as one
+# step inside the CI/CD Pipeline's `lint` job, and nothing ran zizmor at all.
+#
+# The security half of that gap was not theoretical. The first zizmor run against
+# these workflows returned four high-severity template-injection findings, all in
+# release.yml: `${{ github.event.inputs.bump_type }}` and
+# `${{ github.event.inputs.description }}` interpolated straight into the `run:`
+# line of two steps that hold `secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN`. Both steps already declared
+# those values in `env:` and used the raw expression anyway.
+#
+# actionlint moves here from that `lint` job, and changes form on the way. It ran
+# as a bare binary, and a bare actionlint binary does not lint `run:` blocks
+# unless ShellCheck happens to be on PATH -- when it is not, it skips every shell
+# check and still exits 0, so a green result means strictly less than it appears
+# to. Verified both ways on this repository: without ShellCheck installed the
+# binary exits 0 on these workflows; with it installed it reports SC1073 inside a
+# `run:` block. The Docker image bundles ShellCheck and pyflakes, so the check
+# cannot quietly narrow itself. The `-ignore` flag the step used to carry now
+# lives in `.github/actionlint.yaml`, which the image reads as well.
+#
+# Both jobs are gated on `paths: ['.github/**']`: their findings can only change
+# when the workflows do.
+
